Jorgen Grahn wrote:
> Something occured to me the other day: there are dozens and dozens of weapon
> types in the game which I never use.
> 
> I use whatever my character starts out with, plus:
> - daggers (wielded and thrown)

Monks and Priests are restricted in daggers; for those two classes it is
no good choice to use daggers.

> - darts (if I don't have enough daggers, or if I can poison them)

Barbarian, Caveman, Knights, Monks, Samurais and Valkyries are restricted
in darts.

> - bow and arrow (rarely, unless a ranger)
> - long sword
> - silver saber
> - unicorn horn (against rust monsters & friends)

Etc etc. There are roles where weapons are restricted so I don't use them.
If the weapon proficiency can only be enhanced to basic then I only rarely
use them. An exception to that is the early game where I use restricted
throwing weapon types to kill monsters that are slow or immobile to get
their resistances or any other intrinsics (as from floating eyes).

> If I find any of these, I stick with what I have, even if that is just
> daggers or a short sword:
> - knives
> - axes
> - two-handed swords

If I start with one then I often use it until I get an artifact.

> - blunt weapons
> - polearms and spears

Polearms are good for ranged attacks; mainly for specific situations, IMO.

> - crossbows
> - throwing stars

I use them if I find them. But wouldn't enhance that proficiency.

> I find that they aren't worth the trouble, especially when you have to work
> your weapon skills in a direction which will be useful later in the game.
> 
> Crossbow arrows and throwing stars are too rare for me to bother with them.

The gnomish mines are full of crossbow bolts.

> My question is: does everyone use the same weapon strategy as I do?

Definitely not.

> And do any of you run around in mid- or deep-level games with anything but
> artifact swords, the other major artifact weapons, and heavily enchanted
> silver sabers? 

One problem is that (too) many artifacts in Nethack are long swords. So
that is indeed the primary choice for many if not most classes.

> Does anyone bother making crysknives these days?

Yes indeed; rarely, but yes. A Crysknife is still an excellent weapon.
It has d10 base damage against small and large creatures and +3 to hit!
Caveman, Ranger, Samurai, Tourist, Wizard can get skilled in knife, and
Healer, Rogue even expert.

